# P2P API testing

## 1. Oracle

`docs/API.md` and `/openapi.json` define what is right. When the API disagrees, keep the documented expectation and fail — never change the expectation to match the API; report it. What neither document says lives in `docs/QUESTIONS.md` as assumed: do not assert it until a human answers.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — assert documented inactive_vendor_gate
expect(res.status()).toBe(400);
expect(body.rule).toBe('inactive_vendor_gate');

// ❌ BAD — loosen the expectation because the API returned something else
expect([400, 201]).toContain(res.status());
```

## 2. Black box

Never read or change `src/`. Tests talk to HTTP only.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
await request.post('/purchase-orders', { data: { vendor_id: id, line_items: [...] } });

// ❌ BAD
import { createPo } from '../../src/store';
```

## 3. Requests

Use Playwright's `request` fixture with paths only. Host comes from `baseURL`. Never hard-code a URL.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
await request.get('/vendors');

// ❌ BAD
await request.get('http://localhost:8000/vendors');
```

## 4. Data

Every test creates what it needs — its own vendor when checking exposure, unique invoice numbers with `Date.now()`. Never call `/admin/reset` in a test. Never depend on another test's data, except inside one serial flow.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
const vendor = await request.post('/vendors', { data: { name: `Acme-${Date.now()}` } });
const invNo = `INV-${Date.now()}`;

// ❌ BAD
await request.post('/admin/reset');
const invNo = 'INV-1'; // shared / colliding
```

## 5. Per endpoint

For each endpoint: positive (it works), negative (one invalid input, one change at a time → 400 `validation_error` or the business rule), errors (unknown id → 404).

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — GET /vendors/{id}/exposure unknown id
test('unknown vendor exposure → 404', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ request }) => {
  expect((await request.get('/vendors/vnd-missing/exposure')).status()).toBe(404);
});

// ❌ BAD — only happy path for POST /invoices, no negative or 404
```

## 6. Assertions

Check the status; for every 400, the exact `rule`; the values that matter, not only that a field exists; the shape with `expectSchema` (P48). A 400 with the wrong rule is a failed test.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
expect(res.status()).toBe(400);
expect(body.rule).toBe('overpayment_protection');
expectSchema(body, ErrorSchema);

// ❌ BAD
expect(res.status()).toBe(400); // ignores wrong rule / no schema
expect(body).toHaveProperty('detail');
```

## 7. Tags

Exactly one tag per test: `{ tag: '@api' }` for one endpoint or `{ tag: '@e2e' }` for a flow. On `test()`, never on `describe()`.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
test('POST /vendors creates vendor', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ request }) => { ... });
test('PO → invoice → approve', { tag: '@e2e' }, async ({ request }) => { ... });

// ❌ BAD
test.describe('vendors', { tag: '@api' }, () => { ... });
test('flow', { tag: ['@api', '@e2e'] }, async () => { ... });
```

## 8. Known bugs

A test for a confirmed bug keeps its assertions and gets `test.fail(true, 'BUG-<n>: <one line>')` — never deleted, never loosened.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
test('duplicate approve rejected', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ request }) => {
  test.fail(true, 'BUG-3: second approve returns 200 instead of duplicate_approval');
  expect(res.status()).toBe(400);
  expect(body.rule).toBe('duplicate_approval');
});

// ❌ BAD — delete the test or expect(200) to “make CI green”
```

## 9. Evidence

Label every claim `executed`, `inspected only`, or `not yet tested`.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
// executed — GET /health returns { status: "ok" }
// inspected only — seed vendors listed in API.md
// not yet tested — overpayment_protection at +$0.01

// ❌ BAD — claim “all business rules covered” with no labels
```
